1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,920
Imagine you're driving at
fifty kilometres an hour.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,580
About thirty miles an hour.

3
00:00:06,178 --> 00:00:08,238
You want to turn the temperature down.

4
00:00:08,778 --> 00:00:10,918
In an old car, you reach for a knob.

5
00:00:11,567 --> 00:00:13,227
You don't need to look for it.

6
00:00:13,667 --> 00:00:16,007
You just reach out, turn
it, and you're done.

7
00:00:16,657 --> 00:00:19,017
Now do the same thing in a modern car.

8
00:00:19,518 --> 00:00:24,878
You look at the screen. Find the climate
menu. Tap it. Find the temperature.

9
00:00:24,958 --> 00:00:28,098
Tap again. Then look back at the road.

10
00:00:28,708 --> 00:00:30,608
It sounds like a tiny difference.

11
00:00:31,028 --> 00:00:32,748
But the car hasn't stopped.

12
00:00:33,298 --> 00:00:37,238
At fifty kilometres an hour,
every second is fourteen metres.

13
00:00:37,845 --> 00:00:40,685
Fourteen metres, with
nobody watching the road.

14
00:00:43,275 --> 00:00:47,015
For decades, we made cars safer
with better brakes and airbags.

15
00:00:47,444 --> 00:00:50,484
And then we put computers
right in front of the driver.

16
00:00:51,231 --> 00:00:53,531
So how dangerous is that, really?

17
00:00:53,885 --> 00:00:57,585
And if it's a problem, why is
almost every new car doing it?

18
00:00:58,163 --> 00:01:01,643
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

19
00:01:02,263 --> 00:01:03,863
It really helps the channel.

20
00:01:06,253 --> 00:01:08,293
Let's be fair to the screen first.

21
00:01:09,133 --> 00:01:13,793
A map on a screen is better than a paper
atlas unfolded over the steering wheel.

22
00:01:14,593 --> 00:01:17,273
A reversing camera makes parking safer.

23
00:01:18,121 --> 00:01:20,301
The trouble is something more specific.

24
00:01:21,021 --> 00:01:23,741
Researchers call it
visual-manual interaction.

25
00:01:24,555 --> 00:01:27,455
You have to look. You have
to understand what you see.

26
00:01:28,055 --> 00:01:30,755
And then you have to touch
exactly the right spot.

27
00:01:31,459 --> 00:01:33,019
A button has a shape.

28
00:01:33,299 --> 00:01:36,939
A knob has resistance.
A switch has a position.

29
00:01:37,767 --> 00:01:40,007
A touchscreen has... just glass.

30
00:01:41,007 --> 00:01:43,847
Your fingers don't know
where the virtual button is.

31
00:01:44,307 --> 00:01:45,887
Only your eyes do.

32
00:01:46,159 --> 00:01:49,279
And your eyes are exactly
what driving needs most.

33
00:01:50,589 --> 00:01:53,909
Think about what a computer
on your desk asks you to do.

34
00:01:54,503 --> 00:01:58,423
Look at the screen. Move the
mouse. Click. Read the result.

35
00:01:59,103 --> 00:02:03,583
That's perfectly reasonable at
a desk. The desk isn't moving.

36
00:02:04,278 --> 00:02:08,178
In a car, the screen wants
your eyes. So does the road.

37
00:02:08,838 --> 00:02:11,178
And they can't both have
them at the same time.

38
00:02:13,818 --> 00:02:16,238
So how long can a driver look away?

39
00:02:16,922 --> 00:02:18,522
There's no magic number.

40
00:02:19,202 --> 00:02:22,282
But in 2013, America's
road safety regulator

41
00:02:22,512 --> 00:02:24,782
published voluntary
guidelines for carmakers.

42
00:02:25,959 --> 00:02:29,019
A task on a built-in screen should
be possible with glances of no

43
00:02:29,249 --> 00:02:33,719
more than two seconds each, and no
more than twelve seconds in total.

44
00:02:34,289 --> 00:02:36,469
Two seconds doesn't sound like much.

45
00:02:37,169 --> 00:02:40,389
At a hundred kilometres an
hour, it's fifty-six metres.

46
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:43,690
It's not a line between
safe and dangerous.

47
00:02:44,370 --> 00:02:46,830
It's a test that a good
interface should pass.

48
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:51,280
So how do real cars do?

49
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:57,080
In 2017, researchers at the University
of Utah tested the infotainment

50
00:02:57,310 --> 00:03:02,500
systems of thirty new cars, for the
AAA Foundation for Traffic Safety.

51
00:03:03,043 --> 00:03:05,663
They found huge differences between cars.

52
00:03:06,443 --> 00:03:09,143
Some systems were far more
demanding than others.

53
00:03:09,518 --> 00:03:13,818
And the most demanding task was entering
a destination into the navigation.

54
00:03:14,691 --> 00:03:17,191
On average, it took about forty seconds.

55
00:03:17,723 --> 00:03:22,623
Forty seconds. At fifty kilometres an
hour, that's more than half a kilometre.

56
00:03:23,709 --> 00:03:27,149
Of course, nobody stares at a
screen for forty seconds straight.

57
00:03:27,829 --> 00:03:33,109
The glances come in pieces. A
second here. Two seconds there.

58
00:03:33,346 --> 00:03:36,766
But every one of those pieces is
a stretch of road you didn't see.

59
00:03:39,356 --> 00:03:43,656
In 2022, a Swedish car
magazine ran a simpler test.

60
00:03:44,316 --> 00:03:48,496
They took eleven modern cars
and one Volvo from 2005.

61
00:03:48,736 --> 00:03:51,916
The old Volvo had only buttons and knobs.

62
00:03:52,542 --> 00:03:56,762
At a hundred and ten kilometres an
hour, drivers did four simple jobs.

63
00:03:56,922 --> 00:04:03,202
Seat heating and temperature. The radio.
The trip computer. Dimming the lights.

64
00:04:03,951 --> 00:04:06,251
The old Volvo: about ten seconds.

65
00:04:06,331 --> 00:04:09,146
Roughly three hundred metres.

66
00:04:09,246 --> 00:04:14,906
The slowest modern car: almost forty-five
seconds. Nearly one point four kilometres.

67
00:04:15,928 --> 00:04:17,828
Same jobs. Same speed.

68
00:04:17,908 --> 00:04:20,128
More than four times the distance.

69
00:04:20,733 --> 00:04:24,213
But a brand-new Dacia took about
thirteen and a half seconds.

70
00:04:24,293 --> 00:04:26,753
And so did a brand-new Volvo.

71
00:04:27,508 --> 00:04:30,148
What mattered wasn't
how modern the car was.

72
00:04:30,648 --> 00:04:33,248
It was how much attention
the interface demanded.

73
00:04:36,348 --> 00:04:39,168
We already tell drivers not
to look at their phones.

74
00:04:39,921 --> 00:04:42,841
A dashboard screen has
the same basic problem.

75
00:04:43,541 --> 00:04:45,701
Your eyes are somewhere
other than the road.

76
00:04:46,098 --> 00:04:48,758
That doesn't mean the two
are equally dangerous.

77
00:04:49,678 --> 00:04:52,018
A car's screen usually
sits closer to your line of

78
00:04:52,248 --> 00:04:55,158
sight, and some functions
lock while you're driving.

79
00:04:55,596 --> 00:04:58,096
But the road doesn't
care why you looked away.

80
00:05:00,626 --> 00:05:02,726
So why not just talk to the car?

81
00:05:03,495 --> 00:05:05,495
Voice control helps a lot.

82
00:05:05,975 --> 00:05:07,595
You don't have to look at anything.

83
00:05:08,029 --> 00:05:10,469
But voice control
doesn't solve everything.

84
00:05:11,189 --> 00:05:14,729
The same researchers in Utah
have spent years studying this.

85
00:05:15,295 --> 00:05:18,615
Even with their eyes on the road,
drivers busy with a demanding

86
00:05:18,845 --> 00:05:23,195
voice task react more slowly,
and notice less around them.

87
00:05:24,169 --> 00:05:26,129
Hands-free doesn't mean distraction-free.

88
00:05:26,569 --> 00:05:28,269
It just means less of it.

89
00:05:31,279 --> 00:05:33,099
So here's the strange part.

90
00:05:33,179 --> 00:05:35,299
None of this is a secret.

91
00:05:36,049 --> 00:05:40,209
Carmakers employ whole teams of
engineers who study exactly this.

92
00:05:40,474 --> 00:05:44,374
So why is almost every new car
moving its controls onto a screen?

93
00:05:44,829 --> 00:05:48,729
Because from the carmaker's side,
a touchscreen makes a lot of sense.

94
00:05:49,426 --> 00:05:51,466
Every physical button is a part.

95
00:05:52,066 --> 00:05:55,066
It has to be designed,
tested, wired and fitted.

96
00:05:55,146 --> 00:05:58,226
And then kept as a spare part for years.

97
00:05:58,955 --> 00:06:01,135
A car with a few dozen
switches needs a few

98
00:06:01,365 --> 00:06:04,355
dozen parts, and the
wiring to connect them.

99
00:06:04,875 --> 00:06:08,215
A screen replaces all of them
with one part, and software.

100
00:06:08,682 --> 00:06:10,482
And software is flexible.

101
00:06:11,162 --> 00:06:15,002
One screen can serve every model,
every trim level, every market.

102
00:06:15,692 --> 00:06:20,452
A different language? Change the software.
A new feature? Change the software.

103
00:06:21,187 --> 00:06:26,307
Some cars even get new functions years
later, over the air, like a phone update.

104
00:06:27,443 --> 00:06:29,263
And then there's the showroom.

105
00:06:29,971 --> 00:06:33,271
A buyer spends a few minutes
in a new car before buying it.

106
00:06:33,931 --> 00:06:36,411
A big, bright screen
looks like the future.

107
00:06:37,109 --> 00:06:38,989
The problems show up later.

108
00:06:39,689 --> 00:06:42,749
On a dark motorway, when you
just want the fan a little lower.

109
00:06:44,015 --> 00:06:45,215
That's the real trade-off.

110
00:06:46,437 --> 00:06:48,917
The same technology that
makes a car cheaper to build

111
00:06:49,147 --> 00:06:52,517
and easier to update can
make it harder to drive.

112
00:06:53,253 --> 00:06:56,093
Nobody decided to make
cars more dangerous.

113
00:06:56,833 --> 00:06:59,173
Each decision makes sense on its own.

114
00:06:59,793 --> 00:07:01,793
The cost just lands on the driver.

115
00:07:04,943 --> 00:07:07,663
And now, some of that
is starting to change.

116
00:07:08,452 --> 00:07:12,592
Volkswagen moved many of its
controls to touch panels and sliders.

117
00:07:12,872 --> 00:07:14,472
Drivers complained.

118
00:07:15,313 --> 00:07:19,593
In 2025, its head of design
called it a mistake, and

119
00:07:19,823 --> 00:07:22,873
promised physical buttons for
the most important functions.

120
00:07:23,507 --> 00:07:26,967
And in Europe, crash tests
have started to count it too.

121
00:07:27,571 --> 00:07:31,611
From 2026, Euro NCAP, the
European safety rating,

122
00:07:31,691 --> 00:07:35,091
looks for five functions
on real, physical controls.

123
00:07:35,859 --> 00:07:39,359
Indicators. Hazard lights.
The horn. The wipers.

124
00:07:39,589 --> 00:07:41,059
And the emergency call.

125
00:07:41,653 --> 00:07:45,993
A car that hides them in a screen
loses points on its way to five stars.

126
00:07:46,699 --> 00:07:50,919
Some of the oldest controls in the car
are now counted as safety features.

127
00:07:53,609 --> 00:07:56,529
So what should a car
interface actually look like?

128
00:07:57,139 --> 00:07:59,039
Probably not fewer computers.

129
00:07:59,299 --> 00:08:02,239
Just computers that
ask less of the driver.

130
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:04,860
Things you need while driving get physical

131
00:08:05,090 --> 00:08:08,280
controls, the kind you
can find without looking.

132
00:08:08,875 --> 00:08:12,435
Information you need to see goes
where your eyes already are,

133
00:08:12,515 --> 00:08:16,095
behind the steering wheel, or
projected onto the windscreen.

134
00:08:16,693 --> 00:08:19,193
Voice handles what doesn't need your eyes.

135
00:08:19,873 --> 00:08:23,013
And complicated settings can
wait until you've stopped.

136
00:08:24,061 --> 00:08:26,681
Many new cars also watch the driver.

137
00:08:27,321 --> 00:08:30,001
A camera can tell when
you've looked away too long.

138
00:08:30,380 --> 00:08:32,700
That helps. But it can't be the excuse.

139
00:08:33,263 --> 00:08:35,183
"Don't worry, the car
will watch you while you

140
00:08:35,413 --> 00:08:37,943
look at the screen" is
the wrong way round.

141
00:08:38,585 --> 00:08:40,385
The better answer is simpler.

142
00:08:40,945 --> 00:08:43,725
Don't make the driver look at
the screen in the first place.

143
00:08:46,545 --> 00:08:51,885
For decades, carmakers competed on
mechanical engineering. Better engines.

144
00:08:51,965 --> 00:08:53,165
Better brakes.

145
00:08:53,619 --> 00:08:58,919
Now much of that competition happens in
software. Bigger screens. More menus.

146
00:08:58,999 --> 00:09:00,199
More apps.

147
00:09:00,308 --> 00:09:01,808
And that's impressive.

148
00:09:02,148 --> 00:09:03,768
But a car isn't a laptop.

149
00:09:04,651 --> 00:09:09,071
A laptop can ask for all of
your attention. A car can't.

150
00:09:09,292 --> 00:09:14,012
Because the most important task in
your car isn't the infotainment system.

151
00:09:14,193 --> 00:09:18,873
It's driving. And its interface is the
whole world outside your windscreen.

152
00:09:20,161 --> 00:09:23,521
So maybe the best dashboard isn't
the one with the biggest screen.

153
00:09:23,846 --> 00:09:26,646
It's the one you can use
without looking at it.

154
00:09:27,006 --> 00:09:29,546
Because at a hundred
kilometres an hour, every

155
00:09:29,776 --> 00:09:32,839
second you look away
is twenty-eight metres.

156
00:09:32,939 --> 00:09:34,869
And that's the price of every glance.
